1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.200
Above ground, a city keeps moving.

2
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:16.400
Cars cross bridges.

3
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:24.600
Trains arrive.

4
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:32.800
People sleep in buildings that have stood for decades.

5
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:39.667
A tunnel boring machine, or TBM, is one of the most demanding tools in modern construction.

6
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:46.533
India is using these machines on major rail projects.

7
00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:53.400
Large, specialised machines and their parts can involve complicated international supply chains.

8
00:00:53.400 --> 00:01:01.200
This is not a story of one door slammed shut.

9
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:09.000
It is a story of dependence on a few specialised machines, and the engineering needed to work underground.

10
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.120
Start with a spoon pushed into a bowl of wet sand.

11
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:19.240
The spoon removes material, but the sand can collapse into the gap.

12
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:24.360
Now imagine doing that beneath roads and buildings.

13
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.480
Excavation is only half the problem.

14
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:34.600
The other half is keeping the surrounding ground stable.

15
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:40.250
At the front of the machine is a rotating cutterhead.

16
00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:45.900
It breaks or scrapes away the material ahead.

17
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:51.550
Behind it, systems carry the excavated material away.

18
00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:57.200
But a tunnel cannot be left as an unsupported hole.

19
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.850
Depending on ground conditions and machine design, pressure at the excavation face must be carefully managed.

20
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:08.500
On the Mumbai machines, pressurised slurry does that job.

21
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:15.300
Think of holding a hand against a door while someone pushes from the other side.

22
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:22.100
If the opposing forces are badly balanced, the door moves.

23
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:28.900
In tunnelling, the equivalent imbalance can allow the ground to shift.

24
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:35.700
Engineers monitor pressure, movement and the behaviour of the soil or rock continuously.

25
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.600
Now look behind the cutterhead.

26
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:45.500
As the machine advances, precast concrete segments can be assembled into rings.

27
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:50.400
These rings become the tunnel lining.

28
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.300
Imagine constructing the walls of a corridor immediately after carving out the space for it.

29
00:02:55.300 --> 00:03:00.200
The machine is not just digging a hole.

30
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.100
It is helping create a supported passage.

31
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.650
In July twenty twenty-six, the railway ministry reported a second tunnel boring machine starting work.

32
00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:16.200
It is on the Mumbai to Ahmedabad bullet train line.

33
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.750
Seven kilometres of that ten kilometre stretch run under Thane Creek.

34
00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:27.300
The scale is impressive.

35
00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:32.850
The more interesting fact is the coordination.

36
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:38.400
Geology, machine control, surveying, segments, transport and safety all have to work together.

37
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.060
Here is a fictional scenario.

38
00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:47.720
A cutterhead encounters unexpectedly different ground.

39
00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:52.380
The machine cannot simply push harder without understanding the consequences.

40
00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:57.040
Operators adjust the excavation process, monitor movement and protect the ground above.

41
00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.700
Progress becomes a question of control, not brute force.

42
00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:20.000
India's next challenge is deepening domestic capability in high-value machinery, specialised components, manufacturing, maintenance and engineering know-how.

43
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:28.350
The machine makes a tunnel.

44
00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:36.700
The industry behind the machine determines how easily the next tunnel can be built, repaired, adapted and completed.
